claims.claim:
Persistence mixes at least four encoding styles:
- memory images: ObMacroBlockCommonHeader via reinterpret_cast (its comment says 'DO NOT use virtual function'), and ObServerSuperBlockHeader via MEMCPY(this, sizeof);
- big-endian fixed-width encode_iN: slog batch header, tablet version/length;
- unis varint envelopes: 61 OB_UNIS_VERSION in 33 files, 46 OB_SERIALIZE_MEMBER in 26 files, 155 LST_DO_CODE;
- per-field MEMCPY: linked macro block header.
The area also has 18 packed structs, 39 on-disk *Header structs and 63 version constants.

Byte compatibility reaches into oblib value formats:
- the flat row writer copies raw ObDatum payload bytes (datum.ptr_, datum.len_) into the block;
- ObColumnHeader stores ObObjType as one byte;
- ObSSTableMacroBlockHeader MEMCPYs arrays of ObObjMeta and ObOrderType;
- the sstable meta persists ObCompressorType.

Memory handling uses allocators everywhere and no plain `new`:
- 0 `= new T(` expressions, 320 placement-news, 432 explicit destructor calls (`->~Ob`/`.~Ob`);
- 1,308 lines with ObIAllocator& or * parameters;
- ObArenaAllocator on 719 lines in 219 files;
- 750 reinterpret_casts and 211 const_casts;
- 72 unions and 57 zero-length trailing arrays.

The area makes heavy use of concurrency primitives:
- ATOMIC_* macros on 868 lines; ATOMIC_LOAD/STORE on 505 lines in 113 files;
- CAS on 64 lines;
- ObSpinLock 150, lib::ObMutex 91, SpinRWLock 50, bucket locks 14;
- lock guards on 410 lines in 75 files;
- 28 ObTimerTask subclasses, 21 ObITask and 13 dag subclasses.

survey.structure_call:
Split. The upper half translates one-to-one into ordinary Rust once shared types exist: compaction policy and merge tasks, ddl/lob/tmp_file control flow, ls services, the ObIDag/ObITask families, and the int-ret/OB_FAIL error style, which maps directly onto Result and `?`. It is plain C-with-classes code with shallow inheritance (591 of 2,019 classes derive, 12 use multiple inheritance). The memory and format core does not translate one-to-one into safe, idiomatic Rust; it forces a redesign:
- tablets deserialized into pooled buffers with pointers into themselves, plus type_info-driven wash;
- hand-rolled refcount guards and handles (1,122 handle-type lines in 186 files);
- a memtable B-tree with a union-packed spin lock and QClock reclamation over intrusive version chains;
- on-disk headers that are packed memory images, one with a pointer/int union;
- raw pointers swizzled into the KV cache;
- decoders whose signatures take sql:: pushdown executor types.
A literal port of this core would be mostly unsafe Rust and would keep every lifetime rule implicit. The idiomatic version needs index/offset-based tablet layout, Arc or epoch reclamation (crossbeam-epoch style), explicit little-endian codecs with bit positions spelled out, and a storage-owned filter trait in place of sql types. Byte compatibility need not constrain this design: the project itself broke compatibility with v1.1.0 twice (C6).
Files that force the call: src/storage/tablet/ob_tablet.cpp, src/storage/meta_mem/ob_storage_meta_mem_mgr.cpp, src/storage/meta_mem/ob_storage_meta_obj_pool.h, src/storage/meta_mem/ob_meta_obj_struct.h, src/storage/memtable/mvcc/ob_keybtree.cpp, src/storage/memtable/mvcc/ob_keybtree_deps.h, src/storage/memtable/mvcc/ob_mvcc_row.h, src/storage/blocksstable/ob_micro_block_header.cpp, src/storage/blocksstable/ob_kvcache_pointer_swizzle.h, src/storage/blocksstable/encoding/ob_dict_decoder.cpp.

pain_instances:
Defensive code that exists only because ownership is unchecked:
- ObMetaObjGuard warns on handles held for over 2 hours, logs 'obj ref cnt may be leaked' on negative counts, logs 'may be accessed by multiple threads' and calls ob_abort() (src/storage/meta_mem/ob_meta_obj_struct.h:184, 325, 355-385);
- 71 'leak' lines in 23 files;
- 83 ob_abort() calls in 31 files;
- 1,838 OB_NOT_INIT checks.

claims.claim:
The CI init script runs every pinned result with internal tracepoints switched on:
- 10 numbered tracepoints (509, 368, 1200, 551, 311, 555, 558, 561, 565, 408), e.g. EN_CHECK_OPERATOR_OUTPUT_ROWS, EN_CHECK_REWRITE_ITER_CONVERGE, EN_ENABLE_NEWSORT_FORCE and EN_PX_RANDOM_SHUFFLE_WITHOUT_STATISTIC_INFORMATION;
- 2 named tracepoints (ERRSIM_FAST_NLJ_RANGE_GENERATOR_CHECK and EN_THROW_DS_ERROR);
- hidden parameters such as _nlj_batching_enabled, _enable_adaptive_compaction and _max_px_workers_per_cpu.
The tracepoint numbers come from a registry of 322 GLOBAL_ERRSIM_POINT_DEF entries.

Commit e66776e2d fixed three memory bugs:
- the plan cache read a cache object after unlocking the node, while concurrent eviction could drop its last reference;
- a PS-cache callback dereferenced a destroyed item;
- ob_table_columns.cpp used the wrong buffer length.
An internal 'Sanity Lite long-term regression' found them, not mysqltest, and the fix added no mysqltest case. Crash fixes 33bec38dc, dd899675c and 40fcfa901 also changed only src files and added no mysqltest case.

survey.structure_call:
The judge itself needs no translation. The Python harness and the obclient mysqltest client are independent of the server's language and can run the same 272 cases against a C++ or a Rust binary. What forces the call is what the corpus pins. Only 130 of 283 cases are plain SQL. The rest depend on:
- byte-exact EXPLAIN text with cost-model floats;
- OB internal catalog tables polled by include files;
- OB-range error numbers;
- numbered tracepoints and hidden parameters set at CI init.

A structure-preserving translation keeps all of that working: the same errno.def, inner-table definitions, sysvar JSON, parameter seed, tracepoint numbers, plan printer and cost model. A redesign of the optimizer, catalog or error layer would invalidate up to 153 cases (40 CI cases with plan text alone), and the judge would have to be rewritten before it could judge anything. Four generators (gen_errno.pl, gen_ob_sys_variables.py, generate_inner_table_schema.py, syspack_codegen.py) already produce the contract data from declarative sources, so pointing them at Rust output is cheap.

Files that force the call: tools/deploy/init_user.sql, src/sql/monitor/ob_sql_plan.cpp with src/sql/optimizer/ob_opt_est_cost_model.cpp (pinned by the 42 plan-bearing .result files, e.g. test_suite/executor/r/mysql/basic.result), test_suite/fork_table/include/wait_fork_table.inc, src/share/ob_errno.def, src/share/inner_table/ob_inner_table_schema_def.py and src/oblib/lib/utility/ob_tracepoint_def.h.
